  "query_preview": "..\nA weak shortwave is moving from south to north this afternoon\nacross eastern AZ. There is around 500-1300 j/kg of MLCAPE with\nenough share for some multicells and perhaps a transient \nsupercelluar structure this evening. While widespread severe \nweather is not expected, a couple strong storms are possible.\nMain threat would be marginally severe hail and gusty winds. \nThese storms should wane after sundown.\n\nTomorrow will be quite active as the large upper trof begins to\ninfluence the weather in the Desert Southwest. SPC has much of \nthe area, with the exception of far western Pima County, in at \nleast a Marginal Risk (level 1/5) with a small portion of NE \nPinal, and much of Graham/Greenlee Counties in a Slight Risk \n(level 2/5). The main threats will be damaging wind gusts up to \n60 mph, large hail, and very heavy rain. 0-6km shear of 30-45 \nkts and SBCAPE from the HREF of 1000-1800 j/kg will support \nsupercells. With the low freezing heights, and potential for \nsupercells, hail could easily exceed 1\", with some isolated \npotential 2.0\"+ in the strongest of storms. As for the tornado \nthreat, the best parameters are NW of our area into the PSR/FGZ \narea, but we cannot rule out a tornado in our CWA. There is \na 2-5% (probability of a tornado within 25 miles of a point) \ntornado risk for the entire CWA east of Lukeville/Ajo. Best \nchance remains across NE Pinal, northern Graham/Greenlee COs\nwhere parameters are highest.\n\nWhile the severe storm threat is greater north of I-10, the\nthreat technically goes all the way to the international \nborder. However it will be more dependent on if we get \ndevelopment. If we see storms developing further south, they \nwill have the threat for all modes of severe weather as well. \n\nAs for the heavy rain threat. CAMs generally show quick storm \nmotion, but areas that see repeated cells could see localized \nflash flooding. Greatest threat for flash flooding north of \nI-10. \n\nThe severe and flash flood risk could go well into the night or\nall of the night Wed night into Thursday. Another round of\nsevere weather is expected Thursday with a similar \nMarginal/Slight Risk area and all modes of severe weather\npossible.  \n\nDrier air moves in for Friday, though some lingering showers \npossible near the NM border, which could linger into the weekend as \nwell. \n\nTemperatures through Thursday will be at or slightly above normal,\nthen cooler than normal Friday into the upcoming weekend. Latest\nNBM has a freeze potential at the highest elevations of the \nWhite Mountains Sun/Mon morning.",
